WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.049 --> 00:00:04.132
(upbeat salsa music)

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.313
- Hello, I'm Chef Esdras Ochoa,

00:00:08.313 --> 00:00:09.990
and we're at my restaurant here

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.200
at Mexicali Taco &amp; Company.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.130
And today, I'm gonna show you how to make

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.510
our Cachetadas de Pollo,

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:15.900
AKA "Slap in the Face."

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.580
Grilled chicken on a toasted tortilla,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.670
melted cheese,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.410
but today we're gonna do it with a twist,

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.090
our special recipe that we do
with my friend Matt Kim and I.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.130
So Matt Kim is one of my best friends

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.920
that we met a long time
ago in an empty parking lot

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.560
when I started selling
tacos on the street.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.910
He shows up in his car,

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.620
I did not have a clue who this kid is,

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.300
popped up his trunk, pulls out a cooler,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.133
and all of a sudden,

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:36.860
he's like the most
popular kid on the block.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:37.860
This uni starts coming out.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.410
I'd never seen uni in my life,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:40.910
and that's when I was introduced to uni

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:41.980
for the very first time.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.370
So we decided to put it
on everything on the menu.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.983
We put it on tacos, on quesadillas,

00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:47.970
but my favorite by far

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.750
was the uni on top of
our famous cachetadas.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.840
I call it "Slap in the Face"

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.840
because it's spicy, it's crunchy.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.120
It is in your face and it's amazing.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.470
So to start off with
our Cachetadas de Pollo,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.960
we are going to start off

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.290
with our all-natural corn tortillas.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.540
We are gonna dehydrate those.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.240
We're gonna put them on the plancha.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.780
We're gonna sit in there
for about 5 to 10 minutes.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.150
We're gonna go on to our Pollo Asado,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:11.480
and we're gonna start
off with our marinade.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.850
First we have California chili powder,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.540
guajillo powder, onion powder,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.320
garlic powder, cumin,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.290
pepper, salt, and sugar.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.920
So now we're gonna go on with our acidity.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.140
So we're gonna do two portions.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.220
For something like this,
you need about two oranges.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.780
We need about one lime.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.520
And we're gonna give it a nice mix.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.840
So the acidity is very important

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.940
because it helps break
down all the ingredients

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.700
and it also combines them together.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.280
Once it's nice and smooth,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.380
we dunk in our chicken,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.910
and we're gonna give it a nice rub.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.470
For Pollo Asado in the restaurant,

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.700
we love to use chicken
thighs and chicken legs.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.710
We find that it's the
juiciest part of the chicken,

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.680
and it's the most flavorful of all.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.997
So we're gonna give it a
nice mix, a minute or so,

00:01:48.997 --> 00:01:51.210
make sure it's nice and fully coated.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.430
Then we put it in the fridge
and let it rest there.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.710
So while that's resting
and doing its magic,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.720
we're gonna on to our pickled onions.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.800
Time management is so
important in the kitchen,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.440
so we're gonna do multitask a little bit.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.240
So we're gonna take one red onion,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.620
cut it in half, and give it
some nice, fine julienne.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.700
Then we're gonna add our dried oregano,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.580
make sure you crush it very finely.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.910
So what I like to do with my oregano,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.400
I like to grab a good bunch

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.060
and put it in between my
hands and rub it very firmly

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.310
so it turns into powder,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.330
and that's the best way for oregano

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.850
to do what it's supposed to do.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.550
We're gonna a good amount of vinegar.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.330
You could use white vinegar,

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.490
red wine vinegar, apple cider vinegar,

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.490
whatever you have at home.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.470
Season it with salt

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.090
and that's to give it
a nice mix, a nice rub,

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.740
and again, it goes into the fridge

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.170
and let it do its magic.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.320
So food in my family means everything.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.490
It's the time that we all get together,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.450
forget if we're mad at each
other, celebrate things,

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.480
and its all good moments,
good happy memories.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.140
It's a way to remember our ancestors,

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.300
our grandma's special recipes
that are true to our family.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.310
Mexicali, it is actually the place

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.620
where I was raised and grew up

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.700
down in Mexicali, Baja California.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.300
I learned a lot about
hard work and dedication.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.530
I moved to LA and I decided

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.210
that when I opened my first
restaurant or taco stand,

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.740
I was gonna name it after my hometown

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.460
of Mexicali, Baja California,

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.260
and a lot of the influence
with its cuisine,

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.880
we showcase here at the restaurant.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.550
And I think it's proper
that we name it Mexicali.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.560
One of the most important
parts of our cachetada

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.210
is actually our signature sauce

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.780
that goes inside, Chipotle Aioli sauce.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:21.720
It sounds fancy, but it's
really just mayonnaise

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.510
with chipotle and a
little bit of seasoning.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.000
We're gonna get our mayonnaise.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.570
We're gonna add a good
amount of chipotle sauce.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.830
If you like it spicy with
a with a kick the way I do,

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.920
I would add extra
chipotle peppers in there.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.010
We're gonna add a little
bit of salt to even it out,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.310
and we're gonna add a
good amount of lime juice.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.560
Give it a nice whisk so
it's nice and smooth.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.880
And if you're like me,
there's never enough heat.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.460
Put some more of that
chipotle on your aioli.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.070
And then, again, put it aside.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.130
So our chicken's been marinating
about 15 to 20 minutes.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.360
We're gonna grab our
chicken, put it on the grill.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.190
You want to go medium on this one.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.450
If you go too high, you're
gonna burn the outside,

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:56.960
and the inside is not gonna cook.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.030
Now, it's very important when
you're grilling your chicken,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.890
you got to flip it multiple
times back and forth.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:07.060
Look for those beautiful grill
marks, for the smoke signals,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.860
for the fire bursting
everywhere on the grill.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.880
It's not like a steak.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.980
Steak, you let it sit there, high heat,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.030
flip it once, maybe, and
then it's ready to go

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.220
with chicken you have to
ready dedicate some time

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.340
and give it some care.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.040
I usually want to get that nice char.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.310
Oh, that looks so good.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.860
So now the chicken is grilling,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.640
Matt is gonna do his thing.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:25.680
He's so excited.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.830
This is what he lives for.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.130
So he's gonna crack it open.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.460
He's got this really
cool device that he uses,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.630
and then he's gonna
scoop out the golden uni.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:33.950
So he takes the uni,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.560
he sits it in water to
rinse it off a little bit

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.870
and then it's ready to go.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:38.703
What's so cool about Matt

00:04:38.703 --> 00:04:41.340
is that he's just as adventurous as I am.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.460
This guy will go rock climbing,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.410
scuba diving, spear fishing,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:46.830
all the things that I love to do

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.170
but I don't really have the guts to.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:49.320
So I kind of put him to the test.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.460
First he gets it done,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.430
then I say, "Okay, it's
doable, let's jump on it."

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.280
So we have an amazing relationship.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.890
We know each other very, very well,

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.300
especially when it comes to food.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.000
So we're always eating out, hanging out,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.220
and reminiscing, and doing
new adventures all the time.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.480
So now everything is coming together.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.650
We are getting very close to the end.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:05.640
We're gonna grab our chicken,

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.850
take it off the heat,

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.947
and we're gonna let it rest
just as you would expect.

00:05:08.947 --> 00:05:10.600
Set it aside for 5 to 10 minutes.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.960
While that's doing its thing,

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.230
we're gonna grab our tostadas,
nice and dehydrated, crispy,

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.063
but we're not done yet.

00:05:16.063 --> 00:05:18.010
We're gonna load it up
with Monterey Jack Cheese.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.510
We're gonna put them on the grill,

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:20.343
and we're looking

00:05:20.343 --> 00:05:22.530
for those beautiful, gooey, cheesy,

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.110
bubbly signs of cheese heaven.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.400
Our cheese is doing its magical gooeyness.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.730
We're gonna go on to our chicken.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:29.563
It's been rested.

00:05:29.563 --> 00:05:31.710
We're gonna slice it into cubes,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.680
I would say, half an inch both ways.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.290
I like to cut it in perfect cubes.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.690
It's for presentation, and
it's for a really good bite.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:39.440
So now it's time to put this all together.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.176
So we're gonna load our
tostada with chicken

00:05:41.176 --> 00:05:43.960
so each bite is as perfect as the first.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.250
So now we're gonna go on
and dress our cachetada

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.070
with our delicious aioli sauce.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:49.950
And now it's time to customize it.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.050
This is where the Mexicali style comes in.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.670
We're known for customization.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:54.950
Every palate is different.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.820
So finally, now it's time to
enjoy this with my friend Matt.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.270
- What are you gonna put on it?

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.590
- The usual, everything.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.910
I personally like to
have some chile de arbol.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.610
It's spicy, it's smoky.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.390
I like to put some (speaks
in foreign language),

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.700
some avocado sauce.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.860
And I like to put a little
bit of cabbage for bite,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.060
for a nice texture.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.120
And then we're gonna add
some pickled red onions.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.540
These are my absolute favorite.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.690
I put them on everything.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.710
And then, this is where Matt Kim comes in

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:21.520
and he's gonna show off his
skills with our golden uni.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.560
And if you're gonna go with uni,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.000
might as well go all out.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.530
You gotta make it rain and
Matt does that very, very well.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.970
So we're gonna load it up
with uni, just how we like it.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.880
This brings back so much memories.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.510
- This is the start of our friendship.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:34.570
- Salud, cheers.
- Salud.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:37.690
- Each bite is like ocean
meets Mexicali's streets,

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:40.660
each memories, fun times with Matt and I.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.307
Ah, it is my favorite thing to eat.

00:06:42.307 --> 00:06:43.730
The uni, the ocean, I mean,

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.940
it's your story with my story
put together on a bite, dude,

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:46.810
this is-
- Perfect.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.470
- This is us man, right here.
(Matt laughs)

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.560
So we are all done.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.080
I hope you guys love the
cachetadas as much as I do.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:53.913
Try it at home.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.378
It really is a slap in the face.

00:06:55.378 --> 00:06:56.330
(bell dings)

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:57.553
- [Man] Oh yes.

00:06:57.553 --> 00:06:59.060
- [Crowd] Oh.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.300
- [Man] Nice, there you go.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.128
(crowd talking indistinctly)

00:07:02.128 --> 00:07:05.211
(upbeat salsa music)

